\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\qc\plain\b\fs28 ABSTRACT\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Systems built from several language-model agents (a researcher, a solver, a verifier and a summariser) can answer harder questions than one model, but every extra call costs tokens and time. Fixed pipelines call every agent on every task and spend much of that budget on tasks that did not need it. This project asks whether a learned controller can decide, step by step, which agent to call next and when to stop, so that answer quality is maximised after a cost penalty.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 The problem is formulated as a Markov decision process with a 16-number observation, six masked actions and a reward equal to answer quality minus a scaled cost. A Double Deep Q-Network (DDQN) is trained in a simulator and compared with a single agent, a fixed pipeline and hand-tuned rule routers on 10,000 unseen tasks, with paired comparisons and decision rules written before the experiments. The DDQN reaches a mean return of +0.189 against +0.173 for the best rule (+9.3%), +0.153 for a single agent and +0.003 for the fixed pipeline. It keeps its advantage in five of six shifted task distributions and beats the best rule by 15.7% when difficulty and confidence signals are hidden. Training without the cost penalty collapses (return change -0.31).\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 A second study replays recorded outputs of two real models (qwen2.5:3b and gpt-oss-20b) on 50 tasks. A controller trained only in the simulator fails on research questions because it never calls the researcher; retraining on recorded traces repairs this and ties a tuned rule on the smaller model, but a rule with task-specific options is slightly better on the larger one. A live dashboard lets two controllers race on the same task. The results support learned orchestration as an automatic way to find cost-aware policies and show where hand-tuned rules stay competitive.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Keywords:} reinforcement learning; Double DQN; multi-agent language model systems; cost-aware orchestration; sim-to-real transfer.\par
