\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 1.1 Background and Motivation\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Applications built on large language models (LLMs) increasingly split one task across specialised agents: a researcher retrieves evidence, a solver drafts an answer, a verifier checks it and a summariser formats it [1], [2], [3]. Such pipelines can raise accuracy, but each call consumes tokens, time and money. A single agent is cheap but cannot recover from its own mistakes; a fixed pipeline calls every agent on every task. Our measurements show the second is not automatically better: in the simulator of this project the fixed pipeline earns a mean return of +0.003, no better than doing nothing, against +0.153 for a single agent, and on recorded outputs of two real models it also scored below the single agent.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 The right amount of effort depends on the task and on what has already happened (for example whether a check passed). Choosing the next action from the current situation, with a reward that arrives only at the end, is a sequential decision problem, the setting reinforcement learning (RL) was designed for [4].\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 1.2 Problem Statement and Objectives\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Given a task and a cost budget, choose a sequence of agent calls ending in STOP that maximises expected answer quality minus a penalty proportional to total cost. The controller sees an observation (task type, whether an answer exists, the last check result, call counts, money and steps left) but not the hidden correctness of the current answer. The objectives are: (1) formulate the problem as a Markov decision process with a loophole-tested reward; (2) implement a Double DQN controller and fair baselines; (3) test with pre-registered rules whether learning beats the best hand-written rule in a simulator, under shift and with signals hidden; (4) repeat the comparison on recorded real-model outputs and measure the simulation-to-reality gap; (5) provide a live demonstration in which two controllers handle the same task.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 1.3 Contributions and Scope\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 A loophole-tested MDP formulation with action masking and a reward that charges for every call.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 A simulator study with a documented correction history: three flaws in our own first comparison were found and fixed before any claim was made.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 Ablations and a blind variant showing which signals matter, and that the cost penalty is essential.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 A real-model study on two LLMs using trace replay, a calibrated cost mapping, held-out tasks and task-level paired statistics, plus a live race dashboard and 87 automated tests.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 The tasks are single-turn and automatically graded.\par
\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs28 2. LITERATURE REVIEW AND BACKGROUND\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 2.1 Multi-Agent Systems and Cost-Aware Routing\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 AutoGen [1] composes applications from conversing agents that can use models, tools and human input; MetaGPT [2] encodes standard operating procedures and roles in an assembly-line style; ReAct [3] interleaves reasoning with actions such as search. These define what agents do, while the order of calls is usually written by hand. FrugalGPT [5] learns a cascade that accepts a cheap model's answer or escalates to a costlier one, and RouteLLM [6] trains routers that choose between a strong and a weak model per query. Both choose among models once; this project chooses among different roles (retrieve, solve, verify, tidy, retry, stop) one step at a time, with a state that includes the last check result and the budget left, which is why it is modelled as an MDP.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 2.2 Reinforcement Learning Background\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 An MDP has states, actions, transitions and a reward; the agent seeks a policy maximising expected discounted return [4]. Q-learning [7] learns action values; the Deep Q-Network (DQN) [8] approximates them with a neural network, stabilised by experience replay [9] and a slowly updated target network. DQN overestimates values because one network both selects and evaluates the best next action; Double DQN [10] lets the online network select and the target network evaluate. We train with Adam [11] and a Huber loss, and apply action masking so every maximisation runs only over legal actions.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 2.3 Simulation-to-Reality Transfer and Evaluation Practice\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Policies trained in simulation often degrade on the real system; domain randomisation [12] widens the simulator so reality looks like one more variation. We measure the gap directly by running a simulator-trained controller on recorded real traces and then retraining on them. Deep RL results are sensitive to seeds and implementation details [13] and point estimates hide uncertainty [14], so we use five seeds, final checkpoints, confidence intervals and paired comparisons. We found no study that learns a sequential controller over heterogeneous roles under an explicit cost, against fair tuned baselines with decision rules fixed in advance, and reports transfer to real models.\par
\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs28 3. PROBLEM FORMULATION AND SYSTEM DESIGN\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 3.1 System Overview\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 A {\b controller} observes the situation and chooses the next action; four {\b agents} carry it out; an {\b environment} keeps the hidden state, charges every call and, at the end, grades the answer and pays the reward. The same controller runs on a simulator, on a replay of recorded real-model outputs and on live model calls (Figure 3.1). Tasks are of three types: {\b coding} (a small Python function), {\b analytical} (a word problem with an exact numeric answer) and {\b research} (a question about invented facts in 16 documents, which no model can answer from memory).\par
