\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Reward.} R = q x 1[correct] - lambda x C / C_ref - 0.3 x 1[no answer], where C is the sum of call costs, lambda = 0.5 and C_ref = 0.4. The quality q is 1 (0.9 in the simulator for a correct answer that was never summarised). Cost is paid when it occurs and quality at the end, so nothing rewards producing more text. An episode ends at STOP, when cost reaches the budget (uniform in 0.5 to 1.2) or after 8 steps. Four automated tests check the reward cannot be gamed: stopping at once scores below solve-then-stop; a useless repeat scores below stopping; a verified answer beats the same answer with wasted calls; exhausting the budget with no answer scores worst.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 3.3 The Simulator\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 One solver call is correct with probability p = skill x (1 - difficulty) x 0.85^n x g(e) x b, where n counts earlier solver calls, g(e) = 0.6 + 0.4e for research and 0.9 + 0.1e otherwise (e is evidence, +0.35 per researcher call up to 1) and b = 1.10 for a retry after a failed check. Solver skill and verifier reliability are 0.95, 0.85 and 0.70 for coding, analytical and research tasks. Base costs are 0.08, 0.08, 0.10 (researcher), 0.10, 0.12, 0.15 (solver), 0.03, 0.05, 0.08 (verifier) and 0.03 (summariser), each multiplied by a random factor in 0.8 to 1.2; a retry costs 0.8 of a first call. Difficulty is Beta(2, 2).\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 3.4 The Real-Model Environment\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Fifty tasks are used: 20 coding (hidden unit tests decide success; the verifier runs only the visible ones), 15 analytical and 15 research (6 need one document, 9 need two or three). The main model is qwen2.5:3b run locally through Ollama; a stronger check uses gpt-oss-20b through Groq. Research retrieval is plain keyword search; an early version let the model summarise passages and it copied "none of these help" into answers, so research accuracy fell to 0 of 9 until retrieval was made model-free. Agent outputs were recorded once (several solver samples per task and evidence level, with the verifier verdict), and every policy replays the same recordings, so differences come from the policy and not from model luck; a retry replays the next unused sample. Real calls are priced as (tokens in + tokens out) / 2500 + 0.02, so an average solver call costs about 0.12 as in the simulator. In a pilot qwen reported confidence 0.98 when right and 1.00 when wrong, and no non-oracle difficulty estimate existed, so difficulty and confidence are set to zero in every real-model experiment (Section 5.4 measures the cost of this).\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 3.5 The Double DQN Controller and Baselines\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 The controller is a network with two hidden layers of 128 units. Episodes use an epsilon-greedy policy over legal actions; transitions (with next-state masks) fill a 50,000-entry replay buffer. The target is y = r + 0.99 x Q_target(s', argmax over legal a' of Q_online(s', a')); the Huber loss is minimised with Adam (learning rate 0.0003, gradient norm clipped at 10, batch 64) and the target network is refreshed every 1,000 steps. Epsilon falls from 1.0 to 0.05 over the first 60% of 60,000 episodes (20,000 for the real-trace refit); five seeds are trained and the final checkpoint is used. The blind variant zeroes the difficulty and confidence inputs; the refit variant is trained on the replay environment of the 26 development tasks only. Baselines are a {\b single agent}, a {\b fixed pipeline} (researcher, solver, verifier, summariser, stop), a {\b rule router} (best of 960 hand-built rules, tuned on 5,000 development tasks), a {\b blind rule} (best of 256 rules without difficulty or confidence) and, for real traces, a {\b typed rule} (best of 768 rules with per-task-type options).\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 3.6 The Demonstration System\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 A web dashboard runs any two controllers on the same task, live or from recorded runs. The user picks a task and two corners (Figure 3.2) and presses RACE; five one-click shortcuts set up prepared matchups. The page scrolls to the race, shows each side's steps as they happen, and at the end names a winner by the training reward (computed, never fixed in advance), draws money spent per step for both, and offers a scoreboard over 24 held-out tasks.\par
