\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs28 4. EXPERIMENTAL METHODOLOGY\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Splits and statistics.} In the simulator, rules are tuned on 5,000 development tasks (seeds 0 to 4,999) and everything is reported on 10,000 test tasks (seeds 100,000 to 109,999); training seeds start at 1,000,000 and never overlap. For real models the 50 tasks are split by type, even positions to development (26 tasks: rule tuning and refit) and odd positions to test (24 tasks, 100 seeded episodes each). Methods are compared on identical seeded tasks, so differences are paired. The simulator interval is 1.96 standard errors of the paired difference. For real models the unit is the task: returns are averaged per task and the interval is 2.07 times the standard deviation of the 24 paired differences over the square root of 24, because episodes of one task are not independent. A method is said to beat another only if the interval excludes zero, and for DDQN results at least four of five seeds must also lie above the comparison.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Pre-registration and fairness.} Before each experiment the question, settings, predictions and decision rule were written into the decision log and committed to version control; afterwards the log records the result and scores each prediction, including the wrong ones (Appendix B). Rules are tuned on development tasks only (tuning 300 rules on 1,000 tasks once gave a lucky winner, +0.177 on development and +0.137 on test, so the set was enlarged), the rule family was extended until it could use every signal the controller uses, final checkpoints of all seeds are reported without picking, and the blind controller is compared with a blind rule.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Implementation.} About 3,300 lines of Python with PyTorch and 87 automated tests. Models run on a personal laptop (qwen2.5:3b through Ollama; gpt-oss-20b through Groq, capped at 650 requests); training one simulator controller takes a few minutes. The repository holds the decision log, all run logs and the recorded traces.\par
\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs28 5. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Returns are the reward of Section 3.2, averaged over tasks (higher is better). Simulator numbers use 10,000 unseen test tasks unless stated; real-model numbers use the 24 held-out tasks.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.1 Validity Checks and Corrections\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Feasibility tests in simple worlds found the best hand rule within 0 to 1.3% of the optimum (no-go), so a richer world was designed before it was tested. The first DDQN comparison looked promising (+0.173, 12.7% above the best simple baseline), but three flaws in our own comparison were found before any claim was made: the rule search could not skip the summariser; a lucky tuning winner; and the rules never read the difficulty estimate the DDQN reads. With difficulty-aware rules (960 rules) the best rule reached +0.173 and the DDQN advantage fell to 0%; the 12.7% came from a weak baseline. A tested mechanism also failed: skipping the check when confidence is below 0.5 changed the return by only +0.0075 (interval 0.0063), so that hypothesis was not supported.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.2 Training Stability and the Main Simulator Result\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 With the original 20,000-episode setting one seed (+0.136) was clearly below the rule. Three longer settings declared in advance were compared on development tasks: S1 (learning rate 0.001) +0.181, S2 (learning rate 0.0003, target update 1,000) +0.203 and S3 (S2 with longer exploration) +0.198. S2 was chosen on development return alone and removed the unstable seed: its five test scores (+0.183 to +0.195) all exceed the best rule. Figure 5.1 shows the learning curve, which flattens near +0.205 after about 20,000 episodes.\par
