\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.4 Which Signals Matter: Ablations and the Blind Controller\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Each variant was retrained for five seeds and tested on the same 10,000 tasks (Figure 5.2b). The cost penalty is by far the most important ingredient: without it the controller learns that calls are free, makes 7.2 calls per task at cost 0.54, and its true return falls to -0.123 (a change of -0.312). Of the signals, the memory of the episode matters most in the training world (-0.021), because without the last check result the controller stops verifying. The difficulty estimate (-0.010) and confidence score (-0.008) overlap, since both say the answer is probably wrong. Our predictions were only partly right: the cost-penalty prediction held, the difficulty estimate hurts most among the signals only in the research-heavy world, and the memory of the episode hurt more than expected in the training world.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Real models give neither difficulty nor confidence, so both were removed together. The best blind rule is the single agent in disguise (+0.153); the blind DDQN reaches +0.177, 15.7% above it, with all five seeds ahead by +0.017 to +0.033 beyond their intervals, passing the pre-registered bar. The prediction that the DDQN would lose half its edge was wrong: it lost 0.012 while the best rule lost 0.020.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Research questions cannot be answered without the researcher: success is 0% with no call; with one call it is 40% (qwen) and 53% (gpt-oss), and with two it is 43% and 93%. The smaller model cannot chain facts across documents, so it plateaus near 43%. Coding success without help is 0.68 (qwen) and 1.00 (gpt-oss); analytical success is 0.95 and 1.00. Both models already solve most coding and analytical tasks, so orchestration has little to gain there.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.5 Real Models: Recorded Traces and Controller Comparison\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Rules and the refit DDQN were fitted on the 26 development tasks and every policy replayed on the 24 held-out tasks (Table 5.3, Figure 5.3). The first tuned rule calls the researcher once on research tasks, solves once and stops; the typed rule calls it once (qwen) or twice (gpt-oss), verifies coding answers on qwen and retries twice.\par
