\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 A well-chosen rule beats the fixed pipeline on both models (+0.233 and +0.388 with intervals 0.134 and 0.140), because the pipeline pays about three times the cost of a single agent for steps most tasks do not need. On gpt-oss the refit DDQN beats the single agent (+0.207, interval 0.142) and the zero-shot controller is slightly but clearly worse than it (-0.033, interval 0.009). When the rule family may be task-specific, the refit DDQN ties the typed rule on qwen (+0.005, interval 0.018) and is slightly but clearly behind it on gpt-oss (-0.048, interval 0.041): it calls the researcher about 3.3 times on gpt-oss research tasks where two suffice, and one of five seeds scores noticeably lower (+0.656 against +0.79 to +0.81). With only 26 training tasks this is unsurprising. On these traces a hand-tuned rule is as good as the learned policy or slightly better; the learned policy finds a comparable rule without being told which options exist.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.6 The Simulation-to-Reality Gap and Retraining\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 Run unchanged on real traces, the simulator-trained blind DDQN never calls the researcher on research tasks, so its success there is 0.00 on both models. In the simulator the researcher only raised later solver success and was rarely worth its cost; with real models it is indispensable. Retraining on traces repairs the gap: the refit controller calls the researcher 1.40 (qwen) and 3.33 (gpt-oss) times per research task, and research success becomes 0.46 and 0.87. Coding shows the opposite lesson: the zero-shot controller verifies and summarises coding answers, which helps on qwen (success 0.86 against 0.74 for the first tuned rule) but wastes money on gpt-oss, where the first answer is already right.\par
\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.7 The Race and the Scoreboard\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 The dashboard runs the saved refit controller (training seed 0, fixed in advance) against any other policy. Figure 5.4 shows a race on the coding task {\i flatten}: the fixed pipeline and the DDQN take their steps side by side, each recorded in plain words with its cost. Figure 5.5 shows the finished race: both answers are correct, but the DDQN stops after solving and one verifier call (cost 0.109, score +0.86) while the pipeline also runs the researcher and summariser (cost 0.299, score +0.63), so the DDQN is declared the winner. The chart under the banner plots money spent against step for both. Figure 5.6 expands every call, with the researcher's plan and the code produced.\par
