\pard\s2\outlinelevel1\keepn\sb160\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs24 5.8 Discussion, Limitations and Threats to Validity\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b What the evidence supports.} In a simulator whose parameters were fixed before training, a Double DQN finds a cost-aware policy that beats the best hand-written rule by 9.3%, stays ahead under most shifts and beats a blind rule by 15.7%; the cost penalty is the essential ingredient. On real models the choice of agents matters and the policy retrained on real traces finds a rule as good as a human-tuned one in about one case out of two. {\b What it does not support.} The learned controller does not clearly beat a well-tuned task-specific rule on real traces, and it does not transfer from the simulator without retraining.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 The real study has 24 test tasks, two models and a few recorded samples per setting, so intervals are wide and results show direction, not proof.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 A retry replays the next recorded sample, so the effect of feedback on the solver is not captured; the token-to-cost conversion is a design choice that would change the best policy if altered.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 The typed rule family was declared after the real results suggested it (and tuned on development tasks only); the simulator is stylised and its parameters were chosen, not fitted to data.\par
\pard\plain\ls1\ilvl0\fi-360\li720\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa40\fs24 The demonstration model is one training run; retrieval is keyword-based over 16 invented documents, so research results say nothing about open-domain retrieval.\par
\pard\s1\outlinelevel0\keepn\sb220\sa80\ql\plain\b\fs28 6. CONCLUSION AND FUTURE SCOPE\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 This project formulated the orchestration of a small team of language-model agents as a Markov decision process and trained a Double DQN controller to choose the next agent or to stop. In a simulator it beat every baseline (+0.189 against +0.173 for the best rule, +0.153 for a single agent and +0.003 for a fixed pipeline), kept its edge in five of six shifted worlds and, with difficulty and confidence hidden, beat the best blind rule by 15.7%. The cost penalty is the essential ingredient: without it the return falls by 0.31.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 On recorded outputs of two real models the picture is more modest and more informative. The choice of agents matters, because research questions cannot be answered without the researcher and a fixed pipeline wastes money. A simulator-trained controller does not transfer, since it never calls the researcher; retraining on traces removes that failure and ties a tuned rule on the smaller model, while a rule with task-specific options is slightly better on the larger one. The value of learning is that it discovers a policy of comparable quality automatically and handles the cost trade-off without hand-written rules.\par
\pard\plain\qj\sl276\slmult1\sa80\fs24 {\b Future scope.} (1) More tasks and models, to narrow the intervals. (2) Online learning from live calls, capturing the effect of feedback on retries. (3) A randomised simulator in the spirit of [12], so a simulator-trained controller transfers without retraining. (4) A learned difficulty estimate. (5) An LLM-as-router baseline with its routing calls counted in the cost. (6) Performance profiles and interval estimates across many seeds [13], [14].\par
